#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265648 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f9683d9-05b9-5a42-9f81-0170a15dfac1" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Double-Angle Formula to Simplify Ratios"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d81ed476-a3a2-57be-b7f0-4b2de132956d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265650} {:answer/id #uuid "b553aac5-8d44-507e-85e2-483a7ca6ee2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265651} {:answer/id #uuid "08fbb1fa-cd53-52b6-8196-cc14538e4a8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265652} {:answer/id #uuid "820c3579-99cc-58ec-ad98-1a63743b7c86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265653} {:answer/id #uuid "cbe005c4-cf2b-5111-832b-c7b6225601ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265654}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d81ed476-a3a2-57be-b7f0-4b2de132956d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265650} :answer-field/id #uuid "b47f6277-8c45-5320-8eb8-6b1acce1b2dd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265649}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "641c1e03-d0a1-58b9-b0a0-52b1bb02a882" :question/math-academy-id "q-120020" :question/problem "$\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})}{{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{12}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{12})=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can simplify the given expression by rewriting the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ in the numerator, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})}{{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})} & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})}{{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12})} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{12}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
